From d40a40006748e449907196f59a680b947fe1fdb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shogo Agent <agent@shogo.ai>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 09:01:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(api): the calculators answered 200 with a payload of
 nulls
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Both POST /sqftlab/mortgage/simulate and POST /sqftlab/yield/calculate read
their inputs straight off the parsed body and computed with them. A caller that
omitted a field — or used another API's field names, like priceAed instead of
price — therefore did arithmetic on `undefined`: every output became NaN,
JSON.stringify writes NaN as null, and the route returned HTTP 200 with an
all-null payload that no client can tell apart from a real answer. An absent
body threw out of c.req.json() as a 500.

A calculator must refuse input it cannot compute, so both now validate first and
answer 400 naming the offending fields, alongside the shape each one expects.
/macro/scenario already worked this way. Valid input is untouched, to the number:
the client's own payload still returns emi 8893 / grossYield 6 / netYield 5.17.

A negative monthly cash flow never breaks even; that was `Infinity`, reaching the
wire as null only because JSON.stringify rewrites non-finite numbers. It is now
an explicit null.

Also pins DATABASE_URL for the in-process verification scripts. verify-cron /
verify-intel / verify-stream import the real app, so they bound to the shell's
workspace-level default (a near-empty database predating the project schema) and
failed with `SQLITE_ERROR: no such column ...` — six phantom failures that looked
like broken routes and cost a debugging detour. They can only ever legitimately
run against this project's database, so scripts/_env-guard.ts sets it and says so.
A postgres URL (Railway) is left alone.

Verified: tsc clean outside src/generated; 320 checks across 10 suites, run with
DATABASE_URL unset so the guard is exercised; 13 new checks in verify-fixes
covering the validation contract.
