A Portfolio model already existed (richer than the brief's: mortgage, rent, service
charge, a real communityId FK), so it is extended rather than duplicated with a second
PortfolioProperty model. Added buildingName, floor, unitNumber, and — the field that
matters — valuedAt, plus valuationSource/valuationComps for provenance.

THE BUG THIS FIXES

Three seeded holdings carrying `price * (1 + random*0.2)` and backed by ZERO
transactions summed to exactly AED 10,941,214, and GET /portfolio reported that as DLD
market value with a confident P&L. The add route made it worse by writing
`currentValue: num(body.currentValue, purchasePrice)` — a client could lodge any figure
as the market value, and with no client value the purchase price was stored as one,
reporting a flat 0.0% return as a market outcome.

Now `valuedAt` is the provenance marker: written only by a valuation that actually ran.
An unvalued holding reports currentValue null, gainAed null, isValued false, and is
excluded from the portfolio totals. The gain is null rather than 0 when nothing is
valued, because an unknown gain is not a gain of zero. Verified live: the seeded
portfolio now reports totalCurrentAed 0, totalGainAed null, valued/unvalued 0/3, and
AED 10,941,214 appears nowhere.

Other fixes
- The list and the add response disagreed: the list said `currentValue: null` where the
  add response spread the raw row and said `currentValue: 0` — the same holding described
  two ways. One `serialiseHolding` now serves both.
- DELETE is scoped by userId inside the DELETE rather than fetched-then-checked, so an
  authorise-after-read cannot delete another account's row and a missing id cannot be
  probed for existence.
- The nightly revalue is batched by (community, bedrooms) with one query per group; the
  brief's version is one query per holding.
- POST validation added: positive price/size, integer beds/floor, non-negative money, a
  parseable purchase date, and a rejection of future purchase dates. Client input can no
  longer reach the valuation columns at all.
- Pro is capped at 5 holdings on both the list and the add; atLimit is reported at the
  cap, not only past it, so the user about to be refused sees it.

CLIENT IDENTITY — a bug I introduced on Day 7

Account-scoped routes resolve the caller FROM THE REQUEST. PortfolioPage and CmaPage both
used plain `fetch`, so the browser arrived as a guest and the portfolio showed a
"needs a Pro plan" paywall to the very account that owned the holdings — and an
Enterprise user would have been refused by the PDF export button. Both now use
`authedFetch` from src/lib/session, and verify-day8 scans the source so a bare fetch on
an account-scoped route cannot come back. Five sites are legitimately bare and each says
why with a `bare-fetch-ok:` note (sign-in must not present a stale identity; the
calculators and waitlist read no account data).

server.tsx REGRESSION

Editing prisma/schema.prisma regenerates server.tsx and RELOCATES the SHOGO:CUSTOM
asset-routing block to the bottom, below the SPA catch-all, where it silently stops
working — the markers survive, the ordering does not. That took verify-server-routing
from 8/8 to 1/8: assets served as HTML, no cache headers, /api unreachable through the
prefix fold. Restored above the static handlers and documented, since the next schema
change will do it again.

Known platform limitation (not fixable in-repo)

The public edge (preview.shogo.ai) strips BOTH `Authorization` and `Cookie`, so no
account-scoped route is reachable from a browser through that host: the same request with
the same bearer is 200 on localhost:8080 and 403 through the edge. Production is
same-origin and unaffected. Account features therefore cannot be exercised in the canvas
preview.

verify-day6 asserted the old "Coming soon" placeholder and the bare-fetch strings; those
assertions now cover the new wiring.
